WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:05.190
"The Batman" is the latest take on one of
the most recognizable superheroes of all time.

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:09.540
Even the most offhand line in the film reveals
more about the character than you might think.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:10.540
Wanna know more?

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:11.660
Keep watching.

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:13.919
"I'm vengeance."

00:00:13.919 --> 00:00:19.230
Those two words, spotlighted in the trailer,
had already become something of a viral sensation

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:23.910
on social media before the film was released,
similar to a familiar line from Tim Burton's

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:25.080
take on the Caped Crusader.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:26.660
"He blew his light out!

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:28.250
Now you wanna get nuts?

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:29.250
C'mon!

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:30.250
Let's get nuts!"

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:33.390
But what's interesting is that if you search…

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:36.640
#imvengeance
…on twitter, many people have embraced it

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:41.010
in a way that pokes fun at Batman's constant
brooding and self-seriousness.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:43.680
Which largely mirrors what happens in the
film.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:47.219
Although Batman doesn't say the line in the
presence of any of the film's main characters,

00:00:47.219 --> 00:00:49.770
that doesn't stop Catwoman from teasing him
about it.

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:54.149
A few others do as well, treating his declaration
like an embarrassing Rage Against the Machine

00:00:54.149 --> 00:00:56.210
lyric he once scribbled in their yearbook.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:01:00.610
Selena Kyle teases him at one point, saying:
"Come on, Vengeance."

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:04.070
Obviously, certain elements are intrinsic
in the Batman character.

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:08.070
His parents were murdered, and he has since
spent his days and nights training and planning

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:10.290
to become the ultimate crime deterrent.

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:14.670
A big part of his power is the fear he instills
in Gotham's criminals, so if there's anyone

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:18.780
who could credibly utter a line like "I'm
vengeance" with complete sincerity, it would

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:19.810
be The Batman.

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:24.470
But at the same time, Pattinson's Batman is
just beginning to come to grips with the cause-and-effect

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:26.610
his presence has created in Gotham.

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:30.450
The crime is relentless, the villains are
increasingly more dynamic, and his nightly

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:32.990
efforts don't seem to be making much of a
dent.

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:37.280
Overall, there's a sense of near-mockery throughout
"The Batman" with characters like Riddler,

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:42.804
Penguin, and Carmine Falcone treating their
adversary's sincerity as if it's a shortcoming.

00:01:42.804 --> 00:01:44.444
"You've got a lot of cats."

00:01:44.444 --> 00:01:46.490
"I have a thing about strays."

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:51.690
It's a line spoken coyly by Selina Kyle in
response to Batman's less than genuine observation.

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:54.009
It also sums up this take on the character.

00:01:54.009 --> 00:01:58.759
Nearly every action we see from this Catwoman
is motivated by the welfare or memory of someone

00:01:58.759 --> 00:01:59.759
else.

00:01:59.759 --> 00:02:02.680
She's relentlessly compassionate toward the
vulnerable, just as you'd take in a stray

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:04.840
cat and give it the love it needs to grow.

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:09.110
However, her biggest motivation is her mission
to save her friend who has gone missing.

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:13.820
But like Batman, she always has the memory
of her murdered mother coloring her actions.

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:18.100
Then of course there's Batman himself, another
stray that Selina often pushes, cheers up,

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:19.410
and looks after.

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:23.390
Take a look at the scene where Selina agrees
to wear special contact lenses that broadcast

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:28.510
a video signal to work at the 44 Below club
hidden within Penguin's Iceberg Lounge.

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:30.770
Clearly, she hates being an informant for
Batman.

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:34.810
But she's willing to risk her life to help
out the Bat, and hopefully, get some information

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:36.550
on the missing girl's whereabouts.

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:40.890
In some ways, this take on Catwoman might
be the most purely "good' one fans have seen

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:41.890
in a film.

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:45.500
Sure, she technically retains the skills of
a cat burglar, but she uses them to get back

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:48.940
stolen passports rather than steal family
jewels.

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:52.660
Arguably, she's every bit the "hero" that
Batman is, and unless you're willing to bring

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:58.070
up Halle Berry's Catwoman, Zoe Kravitz's take
on the character is the kindest Catwoman we've seen.

00:02:59.175 --> 00:03:03.750
The line is sure to get a giggle from viewers,
and isn't that what the Riddler is all about?

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:05.270
"It's too delicious.

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:08.300
I even gave you a tip-off."

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:11.950
And keep in mind, moviegoers haven't seen
a cinematic take on this character since Jim

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:14.400
Carrey in 1995's "Batman Forever."

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:18.560
More than anyone else in Batman's rogues'
gallery, the man typically known as Edward

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:21.000
Nygma seems made for these times.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:25.170
The modern world is filled with mind games,
fake news, anonymous troublemakers, and other

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:28.950
touchstones the Riddler once manipulated with
a pen and piece of paper.

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:32.450
This is the perfect opportunity to update
the character into something more threatening

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:35.880
and less cartoonish, and "The Batman" leans
into that.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:40.560
"Say hello to my followers; we're live!" he
says at one point, kicking off a live stream

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:44.520
with the intention of holding the District
Attorney accountable for his perceived shortcomings.

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:46.820
"They're here to watch our little trial."

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:51.490
The Riddler's creepy new mask, off-kilter
glasses, and affection for duct tape result

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:55.190
in a threatening new look that no green leotard
could approach.

00:03:55.190 --> 00:03:59.550
His grainy hostage videos and mocking riddles
work perfectly alongside the user comments

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:01.220
in the corners of the frame.

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:05.580
Riddler uses the tools of social media to
create a formidable army that mimics him in

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:07.460
appearance and technique.

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:11.820
While the Christopher Nolan movies and the
Snyderverse both used modern tech to depict

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:15.000
threats to Batman, neither of them thought
to do so via the Riddler.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:20.318
Now, the character has been reimagined as
a Jigsaw-like manipulator for the 21st century.

00:04:20.318 --> 00:04:22.302
"I want to play a game."

00:04:22.302 --> 00:04:26.430
The potential for further development of this
incarnation of Batman's foe, and his "followers,"

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:27.980
seems rife with potential.

00:04:28.655 --> 00:04:30.810
This line comes at a key point in the film,

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:35.290
as Bruce Wayne continues to cling to any loose
string he can find of his core principles.

00:04:35.290 --> 00:04:39.410
He struggles as Gotham remains a city under
siege, and a rising pile of bodies around

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:43.540
him serves as nagging indications he may be
fighting a losing battle.

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:47.760
Carmine Falcone, one of the most despicable
puppeteers in Gotham, solicits the line when

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:50.120
he approaches Wayne and tries to bond with
him.

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:55.040
"Your father saved my life," says the gangster,
explaining that Thomas Wayne once tended to

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:56.040
him.

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:57.040
"You think that doesn't mean something?"

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:01.110
Dismissing the moment as inconsequential,
Bruce Wayne tells Falcone his father did it

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:03.030
out of obligation, not friendship.

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:06.390
"That means he took the Hippocratic Oath,"
he says.

00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:10.470
What you're witnessing here is the final moments
of Bruce Wayne's naive view of the way the

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:11.470
world works.

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:15.930
In almost every incarnation of Batman, Thomas
and Martha Wayne are hugely powerful, hugely

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:18.560
beloved characters senselessly gunned down.

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:20.590
They are perfect martyrs for Batman to avenge.

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:22.410
But not in "The Batman."

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:27.070
Instead, further revelations are to come about
how Thomas and Martha rose to power, silenced

00:05:27.070 --> 00:05:29.500
their critics, and hid their secrets.

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:33.700
In short, Thomas was far from a noble doctor
who treated everyone equally.

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:38.020
As the line becomes blurred between Carmine
Falcone and Thomas Wayne, it eats away at

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:40.330
the very foundation of the Batman mission.

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:44.520
Could it be that Thomas Wayne wouldn't simply
help someone out of professionalism and duty?

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:47.060
Could it be that Falcone was ... a friend?

00:05:47.752 --> 00:05:53.110
For all his fancy talk and Machiavellian mind
games, Falcone is just another homicidal thug.

00:05:53.110 --> 00:05:55.990
This becomes evident in the final scenes involving
the character.

00:05:55.990 --> 00:06:00.300
When he is confronted by Selina, their struggle
leads to him nearly strangling her.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:04.460
Both Bruce Wayne and Selina Kyle have significant
backstories involving dead parents and their

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:06.450
involvement with the movie's lead gangster.

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:10.660
Falcone, however, is a relatively recent creation
in the Batman universe.

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:13.160
He made his debut in a 1987 comic.

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:17.440
And although his criminal activity has often
been a driving force behind Gotham's sorry

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:21.280
state, this is perhaps the most influential
we've ever seen the character.

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:25.720
In essence, without Falcone, there likely
wouldn't be a Batman or a Catwoman.

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:30.220
But during a far more primal moment, Falcone
tells Catwoman "You made me do this.

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:31.220
Just like your mother."

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:33.020
as he tries to choke her to death.

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:37.170
This confession that he did, in fact, kill
Maria Kyle should bring closure for Selena

00:06:37.170 --> 00:06:38.260
on some level.

00:06:38.260 --> 00:06:43.370
Though she's not able to appreciate it until
Falcone is finally knocked off her and arrested.

00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:44.960
Where does Selena go from here?

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:48.660
Like several of the other main characters
in "The Batman," she is essentially a grown

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:52.250
orphan, grappling with demons and ghosts she
cannot ignore.

00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:56.980
The last time we see Selena, her storyline
is resolved, she has no one left to be with,

00:06:56.980 --> 00:07:00.000
and she's heading off on her motorcycle to
start a new life.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.680
Now, it seems, she's the stray.

